在 Electron 主进程中向渲染进程发消息：

```javascript
// 主进程
win.webContents.send('channel-name', { msg: 'hello' });

// 渲染进程 (preload/renderer)
ipcRenderer.on('channel-name', (_event, data) => {
  console.log(data);
});
```

In Electron, `ipcMain.on` handles synchronous or asynchronous messages from renderer processes:

```javascript
const { ipcMain } = require('electron');

ipcMain.on('asynchronous-message', (event, arg) => {
  console.log(arg);
  event.reply('asynchronous-reply', 'pong');
});
```

Here is a CSS gear animation concept:

```css
.gear {
  width: 60px;
  height: 60px;
  border: 8px dashed #555;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 3s linear infinite;
}
@keyframes spin {
  100% { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
```

Add another gear with `animation-direction: reverse` to show interlocking motion.

A JavaScript Promise represents a value that will be available now, later, or never. It has three states: pending, fulfilled (resolved with a value), or rejected (with an error).

Instead of network requests, think of a timer:
```js
const wait = new Promise(resolve => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve('Done!'), 1000);
});
wait.then(result => console.log(result));
```
`.then()` handles success, `.catch()` handles errors, and `.finally()` runs regardless.
